&lt;p&gt;Please join us for the NYC Voices Community Film Festival!&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Presented by Columbia University Mailman School of Public Health&amp;nbsp;FORWARD Community Engagement Action Corps&amp;nbsp;and the&amp;nbsp;Community Health Collaborative (CHEC),&amp;nbsp;The&amp;nbsp;NYC Voices Community&amp;nbsp;Film Festival&amp;nbsp;aims to encourage creativity in communicating about&amp;nbsp;public health issues&amp;nbsp;that are important to our local New York City communities through storytelling, digital media, and the belief that&amp;nbsp;we all have a story to tell!&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join us for an evening of&amp;nbsp;storytelling&amp;nbsp;as we celebrate&amp;nbsp;New York City voices&amp;nbsp;through a selection of&amp;nbsp;films&amp;nbsp;created&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;submitted&amp;nbsp;by community members, as well as a&amp;nbsp;feature documentary presentation and Q&amp;amp;A.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Feature Film Screening:&amp;nbsp;The Skin You&amp;#39;re In: Addressing Racial Health Inequities, a docufilm series that follows a Brooklyn neighborhood as one of its central stories while examining why Black Americans live sicker and die younger than other groups in the U.S., tracing the health impact of the legacy of slavery and ongoing racial segregation.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The screening will be followed by a Q&amp;amp;A with the film&amp;#39;s Writer/Director, Thomas LaVeist, PhD, Dean of Tulane&amp;#39;s Celia Scott Weatherhead School of Public Health and Tropical Medicine, alongside Columbia Mailman School of Public Health&amp;#39;s Michael Joseph, PhD, Vice Dean for Education and Associate Professor of Epidemiology and a Brooklyn native himself, and Robert Fullilove, EdD, Associate Dean and Professor of Sociomedical Sciences, whose decades of work on minority health and public health equity have deepened engagement with New York City communities.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

