&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;What does courage consist of in our age of rising authoritarianism and democratic backsliding? How can we build the capacity to dissent, protect one another, and act on our convictions in times of uncertainty?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In their new book, &lt;a href="https://www.harpercollins.com/products/on-courage-julia-angwinami-fields-meyer?variant=44366661976098"&gt;&lt;em&gt;On Courage&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, journalist &lt;a href="https://juliaangwin.com/" target="_blank"&gt;Julia Angwin&lt;/a&gt; and political strategist &lt;a href="https://ash.harvard.edu/people/ami-fields-meyer/" target="_blank"&gt;Ami Fields-Meyer&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;provide answers, drawing on interviews with more than 100 activists and theorists from around the world to offer practical guidance for resisting authoritarian pressure. Mixing untold stories with history, spirituality, and movement research, their work serves as an invitation to consider what true courage demands of us today.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Join us for &amp;quot;The Courage to Dissent,&amp;rdquo; a discussion with Julia and Ami on &lt;strong&gt;Thursday, September 17, from 5:30 to 7:00pm at The Forum at Columbia University&lt;/strong&gt;. Together, they&amp;rsquo;ll explore how people cultivate courage in the face of fear, what we can learn from historical and contemporary resistance movements, and how communities can form the habits and relationships necessary to defend democratic values in moments of crisis. Professor &lt;a href="https://polisci.barnard.edu/profiles/sheri-berman" target="_blank"&gt;Sheri Berman&lt;/a&gt; will moderate the discussion.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is hosted by &lt;a href="https://worldprojects.columbia.edu/"&gt;Columbia World Projects&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://alltechishuman.org/"&gt;All Tech Is Human&lt;/a&gt;. The discussion will be followed by an audience Q&amp;amp;A. This event is free and open to the public with registration required. Copies of &lt;a href="https://www.harpercollins.com/products/on-courage-julia-angwinami-fields-meyer?variant=44366661976098"&gt;&lt;em&gt;On Courage&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; &lt;/em&gt;will be provided to attendees on a first come, first served basis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An &amp;ldquo;artist of refined taste and musicianship&amp;rdquo; (Strad Magazine), cellist LOUISE DUBIN has recently performed at venues and festivals including Norfolk, BargeMusic, Tanglewood, Savannah, Garth Newel, and Caramoor with Daniel Hope, Philippe Muller, Stephanie Chase, and many other artists. With Stefon Harris&amp;rsquo; African Tarantella nonet she toured Carnegie Hall, Jazz Standard, and Chicago&amp;rsquo;s Symphony Hall. Recital in France at SalleGaveau, Reid Hall, CRR (all in Paris), and the International Cello Festival (Beauvais). She&amp;#39;s premiered 4 works by Roger Stubblefield, most recently his Divertissement for Cello and Orchestra in New York (2023), dedicated to her. Formerly Principal Cellist of Charleston Symphony and Auckland Philharmonic, she now performs closer to home, in Broadway shows including solo cellist at Radio City for the past four years, Baltimore and NJ Symphonies, and other ensembles. Her album The Franchomme Project (Delos) was acclaimed in Canada, UK, France and the US, where Strings Magazine called it a &amp;quot;rare musical treat that unfolds with radiant energy.&amp;rdquo; In 2025 she released Passages (Bridge), &amp;ldquo;an intriguing collection of hidden gems with accomplished performances&amp;rdquo; (Strings Magazine). &amp;ldquo;From the gravity of the Chopin, to the grace of the Franchomme, to the aggressive nature of the final Caprice by Philippe Hersant, her expressive details do the music full justice&amp;rdquo; (The Classical Music Sentinel). After completing her BA at Columbia University and Juilliard, she earned a Doctorate from Indiana University School of Music, where she received the Eva Janzer scholarship for studies with Janos Starker, and two IU Graduate School grant awards which funded her research of Auguste Franchomme in France. www.louise-dubin.com&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Grammy-nominated pianist JOHN NOVACEK&amp;rsquo;s has recently performed in L.A.&amp;rsquo;s Disney Hall, San Francisco&amp;rsquo;s Herbst Theatre, and London&amp;rsquo;s Wigmore Hall. A much in-demand festival guest artist, Novacek appears at Scotia Festival, Colorado College, Stanford (St Lawrence Quartet Seminar), Mimir (Fort Worth &amp;amp; Melbourne), Festival Mozaic, Toronto Summer Music, Music in the Vineyards, Ottawa Chamberfest, Festival of the Sound, Great Lakes, SummerFest La Jolla, Seattle, Cape Cod, Caramoor, Braunschweig, Lucerne, Menuhin Gstaad, Verbier, Stavanger, and BBC Proms. Chamber music collaborators include Leila Josefowicz, Lynn Harrell, Yo-Yo Ma, Joshua Bell, Cho-Liang Lin, Emmanuel Pahud, Jeremy Denk, Renaud Capu&amp;ccedil;on, Truls M&amp;oslash;rk, Matt Haimovitz, Elmar Oliveira, and Dmitry Sitkovetsky, and the Emerson, Ying, St. Lawrence, Miro, Afiara, Alexander, and Tokyo String Quartets. As composer, Novacek has received commissions from St. Paul Chamber Orchestra, New York Philharmonic, Pacific Symphony, Eastman School, Schubert Club, Seattle Chamber Music Society, Seattle Commissioning Club, 5 Browns, Ying Quartet, Quattro Mani, Ensemble Liaison, Manasse/Nakamatsu Duo, Concertante, and the Three Tenors. A Steinway Artist, Novacek teaches piano and chamber music at Mannes School of Music in New York City.&lt;/p&gt;
